course: dictionary
lesson: 12737
title: Dropper
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A dropper is a small tube with a rubber bulb at the top. Squeeze the bulb, put the tip into a liquid, and let go: the tube fills up. Squeeze again and the liquid comes out drop by drop. «Add three drops of water using the dropper.» The word drops in the sentence is a clue: a dropper lets out small amounts at a time.
explain: Look at the words around the word to work out its meaning. «Mum gave me the medicine with a dropper, one drop in each eye.» Medicine and eye tell you this is used for the body, and the drops show it is a small amount each time. Always check by swapping in your meaning: «Mum gave me the medicine with a small tube that lets out liquid drop by drop» still makes sense.
explain: The word can also describe a person. A name-dropper is someone who keeps mentioning famous people they know, to seem important. «Jake is such a name-dropper: he said he met a famous singer, then said he is best friends with a footballer.» The word dropper here comes from the idea of dropping names, not liquid, so look at the whole sentence to see which meaning fits.
fact: A dropper is a small tube, usually glass or plastic, with a rubber bulb at the top.
fact: A dropper takes up a liquid and lets it out drop by drop.
fact: Scientists use a dropper, also called a pipette, to add small amounts of liquid in experiments.
fact: Eye drops and some medicines come in a dropper bottle, so you can give just the right amount.
fact: In informal English, a name-dropper is someone who boasts about famous people they know.
exercise: «Add three drops of water with the dropper.» What is a dropper? | A tool for cutting paper | A large bowl for washing up | A kind of bird that sings at dawn | *A small tube with a rubber bulb that lets out liquid drop by drop
exercise: «Mum put the eye drops in with the dropper, one drop in each eye.» Find and copy one word that tells you the liquid comes out in small amounts. | eye | Mum | *drop | each
exercise: Ella says, «Jake is such a name-dropper.» What does she mean? | Jake keeps dropping his pencils in class | *Jake keeps boasting about famous people he knows | Jake has a very long name | Jake likes reading names in books
test: In science, the class uses a dropper to add liquid to a test tube. Why do they use a dropper? | To mix paint for a picture | To hold the test tube still | *To add small amounts of liquid carefully | To wash the glass tubes
test: What does the rubber bulb on a dropper do? | Makes the tube glow in the dark | Holds the tube upright on the table | *Squeezing it draws liquid up into the tube | Keeps the liquid warm
exam: «The name-dropper told everyone he had met a famous actor, but he had only waved at him.» What does name-dropper mean here? | Someone who drops their books on the floor | Someone who sells eye drops at the chemist | *Someone who boasts about famous people they know | Someone who cleans the classroom windows
